85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (The application was built as part of a Java and object-oriented programming course, so it has two audiences at once. For a student user, it is a working tool that solves a real coordination problem. For the course, it is a demonstration of classes and objects, encapsulation of related state and behaviour, the Java Collections Framework, menu-driven control flow, defensive input handling, and a small piece of algorithmic logic in the form of a skill-matching score.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (The current version runs entirely in memory. There is no database, no network layer, and no graphical interface. Every user, project, application and task lives in an ArrayList for the duration of the session and is discarded when the program exits. This is a deliberate scope choice rather than an oversight: the goal of the prototype is to prove that the domain model and the workflow are correct and coherent, so that persistence and a user interface can later be layered on without redesigning the core. The report describes the system as it stands, explains why it was built this way, and sets out what a second version would change.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
